{%- comment -%}
  snippets/lada-product-card.liquid

  A FORK of snippets/product-card.liquid, not a restyle of it.

  Why fork. The shared .pcard exists to sell other people's cloth under
  Khan Jee's name: it carries the white-label vendor rule (only Dynasty
  and Pasha are allowed to show a supplier name, everything else falls
  back to a KJC SKU), a compare-at strikethrough, a percent-off chip, a
  Khan Jee Seal badge and a pre-order chip. Lada is the house's own
  label. There is no supplier to hide, nothing is ever discounted, and
  a seal badge on a Lada card would be the house stamping its own name
  on its own garment. Overriding six rules off a shared snippet with
  `.lada .pcard__*` selectors is how that snippet ends up owned by two
  pages and safe to edit for neither, so this is a separate file.

  What the card carries, and it is deliberately only this:
    1. the primary photograph at 4:5
    2. a cloth-and-cut eyebrow, 11px caps
    3. the piece name, 15px/500, sentence case
    4. the price, 15px/500, the same size as the name

  No SKU, no badge, no swatch, no rating, no compare-at price. The
  title is the real title: the shared card splits on " (em dash) " to
  strip the white-label suffix off imported titles, which is exactly
  the behaviour Lada must not inherit, because a Lada piece has a
  proper name (Zafraan, not SEQUINS EMBELLISHED WAISTCOAT NA-WC130)
  and any punctuation in it is part of that name.

  Usage:
    {% render 'lada-product-card', product: product, index: forloop.index0,
       mtm_tag: mtm_tag, mtm_label: mtm_label, mtm_url: mtm_url,
       add_label: add_label, options_label: options_label,
       sold_label: sold_label, divider: divider %}

  Every label above is a string the client owns in the section schema,
  so nothing customer-visible is hard-coded in here. The defaults below
  are only the fallback for a caller that forgets to pass one.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign mtm_label     = mtm_label     | default: 'Measured in the shop'
  assign mtm_url       = mtm_url       | default: '#lada-measure'
  assign add_label     = add_label     | default: 'Add to bag'
  assign options_label = options_label | default: 'Choose options'
  assign sold_label    = sold_label    | default: 'Sold out'
  assign divider       = divider       | default: '|'

  # The hover pair. Frame 2 is a second model frame from the same set,
  # never a detail shot. If a product has only one image there is no
  # pair, and .lcard--pair is simply never added, so the hover rules do
  # not exist for that card at all. That is the whole no-phantom-state
  # fix: not a hover that fades to the same picture, no hover.
  assign alt_media = null
  if product.media.size > 1
    assign alt_media = product.media[1]
  endif

  # The eyebrow teaches the filter vocabulary while you browse, so it
  # wants cloth and cut, not a product type. lada.cloth / lada.cut are
  # the intended source; custom.by_fabric is what the existing import
  # already populates, and product.type is the last resort. Nothing is
  # invented: if none of them are set the eyebrow is skipped and the
  # name moves up.
  assign cloth = product.metafields.lada.cloth.value | default: product.metafields.custom.by_fabric.value
  assign cut   = product.metafields.lada.cut.value
  assign eyebrow = ''
  if cloth != blank and cut != blank
    assign eyebrow = cloth | append: ' ' | append: divider | append: ' ' | append: cut
  elsif cloth != blank
    assign eyebrow = cloth
  elsif cut != blank
    assign eyebrow = cut
  elsif product.type != blank
    assign eyebrow = product.type
  endif

  # Two-tier pricing. Standard 38R to 46R pieces are priced openly;
  # made to measure genuinely has no list price, so the price slot
  # becomes a route to the measuring service instead. Read from a
  # metafield if one exists, otherwise from a tag, because a tag is
  # something the client can set himself without a metafield
  # definition. Handleized on both sides so "Made to Measure" and
  # "made-to-measure" are the same tag.
  #
  # A zero price counts as made to measure too. Nine of the study
  # set's worst product cards are the ones printing a real currency
  # symbol against 0.00 because a variant was never priced.
  assign is_mtm = false
  if product.metafields.lada.made_to_measure.value
    assign is_mtm = true
  endif
  assign mtm_key = mtm_tag | default: 'made-to-measure' | handleize
  for t in product.tags
    assign t_key = t | handleize
    if t_key == mtm_key
      assign is_mtm = true
    endif
  endfor
  if product.price == 0
    assign is_mtm = true
  endif

  # Every frame in this grid is lazy, including the first row.
  #
  # The shared .pcard eager-loads its first four because it is used on
  # collection templates where the grid IS the first thing on the page.
  # This grid is not: page.lada.json puts lada-collection third, behind
  # a full-plate lada-hero and the lada-proof band, so on no viewport is
  # row one in the first screen. Eager-loading four 4:5 garment frames
  # there does not make them arrive sooner, it makes them compete with
  # the hero plate, which is the actual LCP element. `index` is still
  # taken so a caller that does promote this grid above the fold has
  # something to branch on.
  assign loading = 'lazy'

  # Never ask Shopify for a width the source file does not have: an
  # upscale request returns the native file anyway but writes a
  # candidate the browser may pick on a 3x screen and then render soft.
  # The native width is always the last candidate, and the smaller
  # steps are only added when the source actually clears them.
  assign main_w = product.featured_media.width | default: 900
  assign main_src_w = 600
  if main_w < main_src_w
    assign main_src_w = main_w
  endif
  assign alt_w = alt_media.width | default: 900
  assign alt_src_w = 600
  if alt_w < alt_src_w
    assign alt_src_w = alt_w
  endif
-%}

{%- capture card_sizes -%}(min-width:1240px) 283px, (min-width:1100px) 23vw, (min-width:760px) 31vw, 46vw{%- endcapture -%}

<article class="lcard{% if alt_media %} lcard--pair{% endif %}">

  {%- comment -%}
    The image link is marked decorative so the name below stays the one
    announced link. Adding a second real link to the same product would
    make every card announce itself twice and double the tab stops
    across a twelve-card grid.

    Its ::after covers the PHOTOGRAPH, not the whole card: this element
    is both .lcard__media and .lcard__link, and .lcard__media is the
    position:relative box, so inset:0 resolves to the plate. That is
    deliberate. Stretching the overlay across the text would swallow the
    made-to-measure link sitting in the price slot, which is the one
    place on this card where a second destination is the whole point.

    With no picked image at all the box is simply left empty: .lcard__media
    holds the 4:5 ratio and paints --lada-panel, so an unshot piece reads
    as a reserved plate rather than a broken img or a collapsed row. The
    Lada shoot is still one photograph long, so this is the state a lot
    of these cards ship in and it has to look intentional.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <a class="lcard__media lcard__link" href="{{ product.url }}" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true">
    {%- if product.featured_media -%}
      <img class="lcard__img lcard__img--main"
           src="{{ product.featured_media | image_url: width: main_src_w }}"
           srcset="{%- if main_w > 400 %}{{ product.featured_media | image_url: width: 400 }} 400w, {% endif -%}
                   {%- if main_w > 600 %}{{ product.featured_media | image_url: width: 600 }} 600w, {% endif -%}
                   {%- if main_w > 900 %}{{ product.featured_media | image_url: width: 900 }} 900w, {% endif -%}
                   {{ product.featured_media | image_url: width: main_w }} {{ main_w }}w"
           sizes="{{ card_sizes }}"
           width="{{ product.featured_media.width }}" height="{{ product.featured_media.height }}"
           loading="{{ loading }}" decoding="async"
           alt="{{ product.featured_media.alt | default: product.title | escape }}">
    {%- endif -%}

    {%- if alt_media -%}
      <img class="lcard__img lcard__img--alt"
           src="{{ alt_media | image_url: width: alt_src_w }}"
           srcset="{%- if alt_w > 400 %}{{ alt_media | image_url: width: 400 }} 400w, {% endif -%}
                   {%- if alt_w > 600 %}{{ alt_media | image_url: width: 600 }} 600w, {% endif -%}
                   {%- if alt_w > 900 %}{{ alt_media | image_url: width: 900 }} 900w, {% endif -%}
                   {{ alt_media | image_url: width: alt_w }} {{ alt_w }}w"
           sizes="{{ card_sizes }}"
           width="{{ alt_media.width }}" height="{{ alt_media.height }}"
           loading="lazy" decoding="async" aria-hidden="true" alt="">
    {%- endif -%}
  </a>

  {%- if eyebrow != blank -%}
    <p class="lcard__eyebrow">{{ eyebrow }}</p>
  {%- endif -%}

  <h3 class="lcard__title"><a href="{{ product.url }}">{{ product.title }}</a></h3>

  {%- comment -%}
    Price slot. Never a compare-at, never a percentage, never coloured,
    never larger than the name. Sold out rides in this line as 11px caps
    rather than as a chip over the photograph, because the photograph is
    the product and a chip is the first thing that turns a wall of
    garments back into a stock table. It also has to stay legible on a
    touch device, where the action row below only exists to be tapped.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <p class="lcard__price">
    {%- if is_mtm -%}
      <a href="{{ mtm_url }}">{{ mtm_label }}</a>
    {%- else -%}
      <span>{{ product.price | money }}</span>
      {%- unless product.available -%}
        <span class="lcard__state">{{ sold_label }}</span>
      {%- endunless -%}
    {%- endif -%}
  </p>

  {%- comment -%}
    The action row.

    On a pointer device it is placed into the photograph's own grid cell
    and bottom-aligned, so the resting grid stays pure photography and
    the button rises over the bottom of the plate on hover. On anything
    without hover the same markup is just the last item in the card's
    flex column: a permanent 44px target, in flow, never a floating bar
    and never a black strip parked across every tablet photograph.

    Placing it by grid area rather than by position:absolute is what
    lets the price sit ABOVE it in the DOM. The shared .pcard had to
    wrap the image in a .pcard__top so `bottom` had something to
    resolve against, which forced the button in front of the name and
    price in source order. Same overlay, honest reading order.

    Made to measure gets no action row at all. Its price slot is
    already the route to the measuring service, and a second button
    saying the same thing is the fifth element this card refuses to
    carry.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- unless is_mtm -%}
    {%- if product.available and product.variants.size == 1 -%}
      <form class="lcard__act" action="{{ routes.cart_add_url }}" method="post"
            enctype="multipart/form-data" data-kj-atc>
        <input type="hidden" name="id" value="{{ product.selected_or_first_available_variant.id }}">
        <input type="hidden" name="quantity" value="1">
        <button type="submit" class="lcard__quick">
          <span class="lcard__quick-t">{{ add_label }}</span>
          <span class="lcard__spin" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        </button>
        <p class="lcard__err" data-kj-atc-error role="alert" hidden></p>
      </form>
    {%- elsif product.available -%}
      <div class="lcard__act">
        <a class="lcard__quick" href="{{ product.url }}">{{ options_label }}</a>
      </div>
    {%- endif -%}
  {%- endunless -%}

</article>
